our SRDs with ready requirements—srd004-ts, srd105-stty, srd110-pr, and srd111-ptx—they're all part of release 15.0 (the complex utilities release), and all their corresponding use cases show \"not started\" in code_status, which means there's work to be done despite the specs being complete.\n\nNow I need to figure out how to propose up to 4 tasks. Since I don't know the actual weights, I should be conservative and assume weight-4 requirements are in their own groups, so I'd put at most one requirement per group per task. The utilities vary in size—ts has 31 requirements, stty has 11, pr has 10, and ptx has 9—so they're all substantial. Following the priority ordering (cmd/pr before cmd/ptx before cmd/stty before cmd/ts) and the principle of splitting implementation from tests, I should propose 4 implementation tasks covering the first groups of requirements for each utility, though I need to think through whether each one needs pkg/testutils as a dependency first.\n\nLooking at the code status, pkg/sys is already implemented, but pkg/testutils and pkg/format show as not started. That seems inconsistent with the recent vidir commits that included tests, so those infrastructure packages probably already exist and the status might be stale. I'll proceed with proposing the 4 implementation tasks for the utilities in release 15.0, starting with the basic requirement groups for each one.\n\nNow I'm breaking down the requirements across the four SRDs—srd004-ts has 31 requirements spread across 10 groups, srd105-stty has 11 requirements across 7 groups, and srd110-pr has 10 requirements starting with group 2.\n\n3 incomplete. Looking at srd111-ptx with 9 requirements across 5 groups, I notice the release notes mention that all weight-4 requirements should be isolated into their own groups, which suggests I need to verify this restructuring is consistent across all the SRDs. Looking at srd004-ts more carefully, none of the groups contain just a single item, so it likely has no weight-4 requirements. For the other modules, the solo requirements in their groups—R1.1, R2.1, R4.1, and R5.1 in srd105-stty, plus R1.1 and R3.1 in both srd110-pr and srd111-ptx—are the candidates for weight-4 assignments.\n\nIf a requirement is weight-4, it must occupy its own task since that exhausts the maximum weight budget. Multi-item group requirements are probably weight-1, so I can pack up to four of those per task. Given the four-task limit and the need to prioritize, I'll treat solo requirements conservatively by isolating them, then group the lighter requirements efficiently.\n\nSince all four utilities belong to release 15.0, I'm ordering them alphabetically as a tiebreaker: pr, ptx, stty, ts.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the decomposition strategy. The planning constitution requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, and for each feature, ideally three tasks: types/interfaces, implementation, and tests. For cmd/ packages though, types and interfaces don't apply since they're executables, so it's just implementation and tests.\n\nWith four tasks available, I could either implement two utilities fully (implementation plus tests for each), or implement all four utilities and defer testing to the next batch.\n\nLooking at the recent commit pattern, I see implementation tasks tagged with R1.x requirements followed by separate test tasks with R2.x requirements. This suggests I should do four implementation tasks now and handle tests separately, since tests depend on the implementation being complete first.\n\nBut I'm realizing I don't actually know what the requirement groupings mean for each SRD without reading them directly. The R1=implementation, R2=tests pattern might not hold across all of them, so I need to be careful about making assumptions about task structure without that context.\n\nSince I can't read the files myself, I'll reference the SRDs in the required_reading for the stitch agent and let it handle the actual requirement text. For now, I'll organize the tasks conservatively by assuming single-item requirement groups might carry a weight of 4, while multi-item groups are likely weight 1 each. For srd004-ts where there are no single-item groups, I'm grouping the four requirements from R1 into the first task.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit and the orchestrator's ability to pick up where it left off after each batch completes, I should focus on proposing the first four tasks in priority order. Following P8, that means implementation work comes before tests, and I'll order by file path alphabetically when priorities are equal—so cmd/pr, then cmd/ptx, cmd/stty, and cmd/ts implementations.\n\nFor cmd/pr, R1.1 appears to be a weight-4 requirement in its own group, which would make it a complete task by itself. But I'm second-guessing whether a single requirement is substantial enough, or if the grouping structure means something different about how weight is distributed.\n\nLooking at the pattern, I'll organize the tasks by grouping requirements within their weight limits. R1.1 from srd110-pr stands alone as a weight-4 task, then R2.1 through R2.3 form a group totaling weight 3. For srd111-ptx, R1.1 is another weight-4 requirement, and R2.1 through R2.3 would form the next group.\n\nBut I'm realizing I don't actually have access to requirements.yaml to verify these weights—I'm making assumptions about which requirements are weight 4 versus weight 1. The task explicitly asks me to list weights from the file, which I can't read, so I should acknowledge this limitation and either ask for the file or note that I'm working with assumed defaults.\n\nGiven the 4-task limit, I should prioritize the utilities with fewer requirements to maximize what I can complete. Looking at the breakdown, srd111-ptx has 9 requirements and srd110-pr has 10, while srd004-ts has 31—so starting with the smaller ones makes sense. For srd110-pr specifically, I'm flagging R1.1 and R3.1 as likely weight-4 single-item groups that would consume significant budget. 3) = 7 items\n\nFor srd004-ts, there are no solo groups, so everything's likely weight-1, with groups spread across R1 through R10. Now I'm organizing these by file path alphabetically—pr, ptx, stty, ts—and proposing the first batch of tasks, starting with R1.1 implementations for each utility at weight-4 each.\n\nFor the first four tasks, I'd tackle the initial requirements across all utilities: pr, ptx, stty, and ts all get their R1.1 work, keeping each at weight-4 for balance. Looking at the vidir pattern as a reference, I could follow a similar structure where implementation and testing tasks are separated into distinct batches.\n\nFor ts specifically, since R1 spans six requirements, I'd split them across two tasks—the first covering R1.1 through R1.4, then the second handling R1.5, R1.6, and moving into R2 testing requirements. This keeps each task manageable while maintaining the self-contained nature the stitch agent needs.\n\nI should also map out dependencies to ensure nothing blocks downstream work, particularly around shared utilities or foundational components that other commands might rely on. The stitch agent will handle the implementation by reading the SRD and required_reading files, using pkg/testutils if it exists or gracefully skipping tests otherwise per the established pattern. Now I'm mapping out the core command implementations: cmd/pr, cmd/ptx, and cmd/stty each get their main.go files with R1.1 requirements, each weighted as a solo task. Now I'm figuring out the SRD file paths following the naming convention, so I can reference them correctly in the YAML task documents—srd004-ts.yaml, srd105-stty.yaml, srd110-pr.yaml, and srd111-ptx.yaml. I also need to include pkg/sys/ for the SIGPIPE handler and CLAUDE.md for project rules in the required_reading sections, then define the acceptance criteria around successful builds, vet checks, and requirement implementation.\n\nI'm also mapping out the use case and test suite files that correspond to each feature, and I notice the commit title format follows a pattern with the stitch prefix, command name, and requirement references—so I need to match that style when I write the output.\n\nLooking at the dependencies, all four commands are independent of each other but rely on the existing pkg/sys implementation for signal handling, so they can all be tackled in parallel.\n\nFor ordering, I'm checking the primary file paths alphabetically: cmd/pr, cmd/ptx, cmd/stty, and cmd/ts—which already lines up correctly.\n\nNow I need to craft descriptions that are self-contained for the stitch agent. Each one should reference the relevant SRD requirements, mention the SIGPIPE handler setup from the architecture, and specify what needs to be created in cmd/<name>/main.go. The acceptance criteria should focus on compilation and basic functional correctness.\n\nSince I can't read the actual SRDs, I'll frame each task around implementing the R-items defined in those documents, keeping the design decisions grounded in what the architecture and CLAUDE.md specify universally for all cmd/ utilities. I need to read requirements.yaml for weights. But I can't read files. However, the requirement_states in the project_context lists the requirements and their statuses. It doesn't list weights. \n\nThe step 3b says: \"list the requirements you plan to include and their weights from requirements.yaml.\"\n\nSince I can't read requirements.yaml, I'll need to state my assumptions about weights based on the available context (release 15.0 description says weight-4 requirements are in their own groups).\n\nLet me just proceed and clearly show my weight reasoning.\n\nHere's my final plan:\n\n## Project State Summary\n\ngo-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment that generates Go implementations of GNU coreutils/moreutils utilities from formal SRDs, verified via differential testing against Homebrew reference binaries. Architecture: specs in docs/, implementations in cmd/<name>/, shared libraries in pkg/{sys,format,testutils,hashutil,sizeparse,encutil}.\n\nGeneration run 44 is in progress. Many utilities are implemented, but release 15.0 (complex utilities: ts, stty, pr, ptx) is entirely not started. The requirement_states show only these 4 SRDs have \"ready\" requirements.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nAll ready requirements are in release 15.0 (ts, stty, pr, ptx). Per P8, I order by file path alphabetically: pr, ptx, stty, ts. Each gets its first implementation task.\n\n## Weight Budget Analysis\n\nRelease 15.0 description indicates each weight-4 requirement is in its own group. Based on this structure, I'm assigning weight-4 to the first requirement of each utility (pr, ptx, stty) since they're solo groups, and ts appears to have multiple requirements in its group.\n\nNow I'm pulling the specific functionality descriptions from the roadmap to make the task descriptions concrete—ts handles timestamp conversion modes, stty manages terminal settings, pr handles text pagination with headers and columns, and ptx generates permuted indexes. These will be verified against reference binaries from Homebrew.\n\nI need to clarify which reference binaries to use though. The moreutils utilities like ts and vidir don't follow the g-prefix convention that GNU coreutils do, so ts should just be \"ts\" from moreutils, not \"gts\". Let me verify this against the architecture notes to make sure I'm using the right reference binary names.\n\nActually, I should defer the exact reference binary names to the SRD since it will specify the correct naming convention for the environment. For now, I'll focus on the task structure itself: each task needs a deliverable_type of \"code\" and required_reading that includes the SRD file and pkg/sys for SIGPIPE handling.\n\nDrafting the task template...\n\nI realize I don't actually know what R1.1 specifies in the SRD, so I should reference it generically and let the stitch agent look up the actual requirement details. Looking at the vidir pattern, the task title includes the SRD references, and the stitch agent will resolve those when it reads the specification file.\n\nI'll use the roadmap descriptions to add context to each requirement—pr handles pagination with headers and page numbers, ptx generates permuted keyword-in-context indexes, stty manages terminal settings through termios, and ts timestamps stdin with relative-time conversion modes. This gives the stitch agent enough context to understand what needs implementing, even without the full SRD text in the task description. I need to lay out the specific deliverables for this task: creating the main entry point for the pr utility with proper signal handling, referencing the full SRD specification and existing patterns from similar utilities, and implementing the core requirement R1.1 that handles text pagination with headers and multi-column layout.\n\nLooking at the planning guidelines, I'm realizing this task definition is too sparse—it should target 5-8 requirements and acceptance criteria, but I'm working with just a few. The constraint about limiting to 4 SRD sub-requirements is noted, but I need to flesh out the design decisions and acceptance criteria to meet the standard task weight, particularly around the build process, code quality checks, and behavioral verification. So this is an acceptable deviation due to weight constraints. I could add non-SRD requirements like signal handling, error handling, or flag parsing, but P6 requires anchoring each task to specific SRD requirements, implying a 1:1 mapping. For a weight-4 task with a single SRD requirement, having just one task requirement seems reasonable given the substantial scope. I'm also realizing I might be making assumptions about weights without actually checking the requirements file—a solo requirement in its group could be weight 1 for other reasons, not necessarily weight 4. Let me take a more direct approach and just examine what's actually specified.\n\nI'm testing whether I can fit multiple requirements per task while noting my weight assumptions, knowing the orchestrator will reject or re-split if I'm wrong. Looking at a potential grouping for the cmd/pr implementation, I'd need to carefully check whether combining R1.1 with R2.1-R2.3 stays within the weight budget—if R1.1 is actually weight 4, adding anything else immediately exceeds the limit, so I need to be conservative here.\n\nI think the safest strategy is treating solo-group requirements as weight 4 each and fitting them alone, then grouping the weight-1 requirements together. So I'm planning four tasks, each covering one utility's initial work: cmd/pr implementation for srd110-pr, cmd/ptx implementation for srd111-ptx, and continuing that pattern for the remaining utilities.\n\nNow I'm realizing there's a practical issue with this approach — when the stitch agent implements just R1.1 for a single utility, it creates the entire cmd/<name>/main.go file with only that one requirement. Since R1.1 might be basic flag parsing and page output while R2.1 handles header formatting, subsequent tasks would need to modify that same main.go file rather than create it fresh. That's workable though, since later tasks can use the modify action to extend the implementation.\n\nI need to double-check the release constraints. The orchestrator only marks requirements as \"ready\" when they're eligible for work, and looking at the requirement states, only the four SRDs in release 15.0 have ready requirements. The earlier releases have use cases with code that hasn't started, but those requirements aren't flagged as ready yet, so I can only propose tasks for release 15.0.\n\nLet me finalize the YAML output now. at the reference binary naming. For moreutils:\n- ts → reference is probably just \"ts\" (moreutils doesn't use g-prefix)\n  \nBut wait, the architecture says \"gts\" as an example of a Homebrew reference binary. Let me check the differential testing pattern:\n\nFor ts, this would be `gts`. But moreutils installs `ts` not `gts`. The vidir example shows it's also moreutils, and the SRD would specify the correct reference binary name. I'll leave it to the SRD.\n\nNow I'm thinking through the dependencies for all four utilities. Looking at the architecture, srd004-ts isn't explicitly listed in component_dependencies, but all cmd/ utilities that write to stdout need SIGPIPE handling according to the rules. Since stty, pr, ptx, and ts all write to stdout, they all need pkg/sys. I'll include pkg/sys in required_reading for all tasks, and pkg/format only where the utility actually needs it.\n\nFor pattern examples, I'll reference existing implemented utilities: cmd/nl for text processing tasks like pr and ptx, cmd/fold for line-based processing, and cmd/vidir or cmd/chronic for moreutils examples. These give concrete reference implementations without overcomplicating the SRD.\n\nSince all four utilities are marked as \"not started\" with no existing files, every main.go will be a create action. Looking at the task naming convention from recent commits, I should follow the pattern of including the SRD reference and requirement numbers in the task title.\n\nNow I'm writing out the YAML structure for these four tasks, being careful with proper indentation and formatting. Each task will specify the deliverable type as code, list the required reading materials for context, and define the main.go file to be created with appropriate descriptions of what each utility does. I'm adding the final acceptance criterion that all behaviors from the SRD requirement are implemented, then moving on to the next task: building the ptx utility for producing permuted keyword-in-context indexes, which will follow the same SIGPIPE handling pattern and reference existing text-processing implementations. Now I'm setting up the stty command implementation with the same SIGPIPE handler pattern, creating the main entry point that will handle terminal line settings through termios, and following the same build and verification approach as the ptx utility. Now I'm looking at the ts utility implementation, which needs to timestamp stdin lines with various modes including relative-time conversion. I need to create cmd/ts/main.go following the same pattern as the chronic reference implementation, and ensure it meets all four requirements from the srd004-ts specification.\n\nFor the implementation, I'll install the SIGPIPE handler at startup to match GNU behavior when piped to consumers that close stdin early, import the sys package for that handler, and verify everything compiles cleanly with go build and passes go vet checks. I'm ready to write the final response now, compiling the project summary, priority analysis, weight budget breakdown, and the YAML task definitions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":43849,"cache_read_input_tokens":11845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":43849},"output_tokens":27,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"44b11257-208a-47cb-bd47-aa0e107f1056","uuid":"1ba22123-f8d6-4993-9de8-c34d12078912"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01TCwG5kZZPn5Dd12635yjdY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\ngo-unix-utils is a specification-driven development experiment: derive formal specs from C source, generate Go implementations via AI agents, verify output parity against Homebrew GNU reference binaries through differential testing. Architecture centers on cmd/<name>/ (one package per utility), shared libraries in pkg/{sys,format,testutils,hashutil,sizeparse,encutil}, and specs in docs/specs/.\n\nGeneration run 44 is in progress. Many utilities are already implemented across releases 00.0 through 14.0. Release 15.0 (complex utilities: ts, stty, pr, ptx) is entirely not started.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nThe `requirement_states` field lists only four SRDs with requirements in \"ready\" status:\n- **srd004-ts** (31 requirements) -- release 15.0\n- **srd105-stty** (11 requirements) -- release 15.0\n- **srd110-pr** (10 requirements) -- release 15.0\n- **srd111-ptx** (9 requirements) -- release 15.0\n\nAll other releases either have fully implemented code or their requirements are not in \"ready\" state. Work targets release 15.0 exclusively.\n\nCanonical ordering by primary file path: cmd/pr, cmd/ptx, cmd/stty, cmd/ts. All are independent cmd/ packages with no inter-dependencies.\n\n## Weight Budget Analysis\n\nRelease 15.0 description states: \"All SRDs have been restructured so each weight-4 requirement is in its own group.\" Solo-group requirements are assumed weight 4; multi-item group requirements are assumed weight 1.\n\n**Task 0: srd110-pr R1.1** (solo group, assumed w=4)\nTotal weight: 4 -- within budget\n\n**Task 1: srd111-ptx R1.1** (solo group, assumed w=4)\nTotal weight: 4 -- within budget\n\n**Task 2: srd105-stty R1.1** (solo group, assumed w=4)\nTotal weight: 4 -- within budget\n\n**Task 3: srd004-ts R1.1 (w=1), R1.2 (w=1), R1.3 (w=1), R1.4 (w=1)** (multi-item group R